    "prompt": "@image1 - CHARACTER REFERENCE. Keep the exact same person, outfit, and styling shown in across every frame. Gender, body proportions, hair and build are read directly from the reference image and never altered.\nBODY + PHYSICALITY — REQUIRED: The subject moves with full muscular control at all times — core braced, every limb driven with intention. Think trained traceur or gymnast who happens to have powers: precise, easy, never theatrical. No rag-dolling, no limp limbs, no floppy weightlessness. Keep spinning to a minimum. Every motion obeys real body physics.\nCONTACT WITH THE CITY — REQUIRED: The subject is not gliding through empty air. They keep making real contact with the environment — soles pushing off brick, palms catching ledges, shoulders grazing walls as they pass. On every contact the knees load and absorb, the muscles fire, the body answers the surface. This reads as parkour fused with web-swinging, never floating.\nPOSTURE PER MOVE — REQUIRED:\nSwing: both arms reaching up the web line, legs drawn together and trailing, stomach tight, spine long, head up — a clean athletic rope-swing line.\nPush-off a wall: both soles flat to the surface, knees folding deep to soak the impact, then both legs firing straight to launch as the arms drive forward.\nWall run: torso tipped about 45 degrees into the wall, feet striking it in a true running rhythm, arms pumping, clothing dragged sideways by the speed.\nApex of flight: arms falling open to the sides, spine gently arched, fully relaxed — released, not posed.\nGround sprint: flat-out run, arms pumping, head leading, sneakers slapping the road, body tipped into the run.\nLOOK — REQUIRED: Real theatrical-film texture. Shot on a large-format cinema camera with an anamorphic lens. Authentic motion blur, real cloth behavior, genuine city haze. Grounded and real.\nCAMERA — REQUIRED: A single unbroken aerial follow-cam. No cuts. No teleporting. The rig physically tilts, banks and rolls with the subject. Always framed wide enough to hold both the subject and the city around them.\nA 15-second unbroken aerial follow shot. It opens already in motion — the subject is high over the city, airborne, mid-swing.\n0–1s: Already in the air, way up between the buildings, the body rimmed by golden light. Tight on their hand — the fingers spread, the wrist cracks forward, a line fires upward to a rooftop edge. The camera immediately eases back as the line bites and they swing forward.\n1–4s: Wide on a residential New York block — row houses, fire escapes, corner shops. They swing in low, only about three meters over the road, arms up the line, legs trailing, body tight and straight. At the low point of the arc their feet just clip the roof of a parked car — a quick real touch, a light bounce, which they use to change direction. The camera leans over about 40 degrees.\n4–7s: At the top of the arc they let go — body opening, arms wide, legs stretching out. Instead of immediately shooting another line they land onto the face of a row-house wall and run it: feet drumming the brick in a real stride, body canted 45 degrees toward the wall, arms working, four or five fast steps up the vertical — then a hard two-footed push, knees snapping straight, and a fresh line fires across a wide junction. The new swing rips them sideways and the camera rolls around 60 degrees.\n7–10s: They drop in low along a tight street jammed with yellow cabs, a red fire engine forcing through the snarl with its lights spinning and siren wailing. About two meters up, body narrowed to an arrow — arms ahead, legs streaming behind. They drop the line, feet meet the road, and they're instantly sprinting — leaning in, arms driving, three or four strides weaving between the cabs, soles slapping the wet asphalt. A cab leans on its horn. They laugh. Then one foot stamps the flank of a taxi, they blast up off the roof in a single explosive stride, throw a line at a high building and slingshot upward.\n\"WOOHOO!\"\n10–15s: Wide — about rooftop height, not stratospheric. They float at the very top of the arc, arms loosely open, body softly arched, totally still for one suspended breath. The sky is full golden-hour — warm orange bleeding into pink across the horizon, huge clouds underlit in gold. They're a tiny silhouette dead-center against that vast glowing sky, the rooftops, water tanks and streets spread far below. The frame holds. Then they start to drop back down.\nEMOTION + SOUND — REQUIRED: The subject is switched on in every single frame. Never blank, never neutral, never checked-out. Their face and body carry real live feeling the whole way:\nFirst swing — eyes wide, half a grin, locked in.\nFeet clip the car — a quick startled laugh, pure reflex.\nWall run — jaw tight, fierce focus.\nLow through the cabs — teeth bared, a thrill-grin, leaning into it.\nCab horn — a real laugh, head rocking back a touch.\n\"WOOHOO!\"\n— totally unguarded, mouth wide, a genuine whoop of joy.\nFinal wide apex — arms open, head tipped back, eyes shut, a face of total freedom. Not blank, not posed — just someone living the best second of their life.\nAUDIO: EFFECTS ONLY. NO MUSIC. Air tearing past. The snap and hum of a fired line. Feet thumping a car roof. Fire-engine siren. Cab horn. Soles cracking the pavement — real impacts. Their real laugh — natural voice matching the person in the reference image.\n\"WOOHOO!\" bouncing off the buildings. Wind shearing as they climb. Then — stillness at the top.\nLarge-format cinema camera, anamorphic lens, tall controlled athletic frame throughout matching the reference, real contact with walls, cars and surfaces wherever possible, distinct body mechanics for each move — swing, run, push-off, sprint — no rag-doll, no floating, hard Dutch tilt through the swings, golden-hour sunset sky in warm orange and pink, big dramatic underlit clouds, subject kept at rooftop height not high altitude, subject tiny and centered in the wide closing frame, residential New York row-houses, one continuous aerial follow, no cuts, photorealistic."
